CAT = "cparegCorp"; SEG = "COR"
Q = [
("A corporation's book income is $500,000 after $105,000 of federal income tax expense. On Schedule M-1, adding back the tax expense gives:",
 "$605,000", ["$395,000", "$500,000", "$710,000"],
 "Federal income tax expense reduces book income but is not deductible, so it is added back: $500,000 + $105,000 = $605,000."),
("A corporation's book income of $400,000 includes $12,000 of municipal bond interest and is net of an $8,000 fine paid to a state. Adjusting for these two items, taxable income is:",
 "$396,000", ["$404,000", "$420,000", "$380,000"],
 "Tax-exempt interest is subtracted and the nondeductible fine is added back: $400,000 - $12,000 + $8,000 = $396,000."),
("On Schedule M-1, which item is subtracted from book income to reach taxable income?",
 "Life insurance proceeds received", ["Excess of capital losses", "Federal income tax expense", "Nondeductible meal costs"],
 "Life insurance proceeds are book income but excluded from taxable income, so they are subtracted. The other three are added back to book income."),
("A corporation's books show $18,000 of business meals with clients, all properly documented. The Schedule M-1 add-back for them is:",
 "$9,000", ["$18,000", "$0", "$4,500"],
 "Business meals are generally 50% deductible, so half of the $18,000 book expense is added back to reach taxable income."),
("A corporation pays a $25,000 penalty to a state agency for breaking a law. For tax purposes, the penalty is:",
 "Not deductible", ["Fully deductible", "50% deductible", "Amortized over 15 years"],
 "Fines and penalties paid to a government for violating a law are not deductible, apart from amounts identified as restitution or costs of coming into compliance."),
("A corporation pays premiums on a life insurance policy on its president, with the corporation as beneficiary. The premiums are:",
 "Not deductible", ["Deductible as pay", "Deductible when paid", "Capitalized and amortized"],
 "Premiums are not deductible when the corporation is the beneficiary, because the proceeds will be tax-exempt. Book expense is added back on Schedule M-1."),
("In December, an accrual-method landlord corporation receives the next January's rent in advance. For tax purposes, the rent is taxed:",
 "In the year received", ["In the year it is earned", "When the lease ends", "Ratably over the lease"],
 "Prepaid rent is taxable when received, even under the accrual method, though books record it as income when earned."),
("A C corporation's taxable income is $300,000. Its regular federal income tax is:",
 "$63,000", ["$105,000", "$102,000", "$75,000"],
 "C corporations pay a flat 21% rate: $300,000 x 21% = $63,000."),
("Corporation A owns 30% of Corporation B and receives $100,000 of dividends from it. Ignoring the taxable income limit, A's dividends received deduction is:",
 "$65,000", ["$50,000", "$100,000", "$80,000"],
 "A corporation owning at least 20% but less than 80% of the payer deducts 65% of dividends received."),
("A corporation owns 10% of another domestic corporation and receives $40,000 of dividends. Ignoring the taxable income limit, its dividends received deduction is:",
 "$20,000", ["$26,000", "$40,000", "$28,000"],
 "Ownership under 20% gives a 50% deduction: $40,000 x 50% = $20,000. 65% applies at 20% or more and 100% at 80% or more."),
("A corporation has a $100,000 NOL carryforward from 2023 and $90,000 of taxable income this year before the NOL. Its NOL deduction this year is:",
 "$72,000", ["$90,000", "$100,000", "$80,000"],
 "NOLs from years after 2017 can offset only 80% of taxable income: $90,000 x 80% = $72,000. The other $28,000 carries forward."),
("A corporation's NOL arising in 2026 (not a farming loss) may be:",
 "Carried forward indefinitely", ["Carried back 2 years", "Carried forward 20 years", "Carried back 5 years"],
 "NOLs arising after 2020 cannot be carried back (farming losses aside) and carry forward with no time limit, subject to the 80% limit."),
("A corporation has a $15,000 net capital loss and $200,000 of ordinary income. Against ordinary income this year it may deduct:",
 "None of the capital loss", ["$3,000 of the loss", "All $15,000 of the loss", "$7,500 of the loss"],
 "Corporations may deduct capital losses only against capital gains. The $3,000 allowance is for individuals."),
("A corporate net capital loss is carried:",
 "Back 3 years, forward 5", ["Forward with no limit", "Back 2 years, forward 20", "Forward 5 years only"],
 "A corporation carries a net capital loss back 3 years and forward 5, treating it as short-term in those years. Individuals carry forward indefinitely."),
("A C corporation's net long-term capital gain is taxed at:",
 "The 21% corporate rate", ["A maximum 20% rate", "A maximum 15% rate", "A flat 25% rate"],
 "Corporations get no preferential rate on capital gains; they are taxed like other income at 21%."),
("A large corporation, one with $1 million or more of taxable income in any of its 3 prior years, generally must base its estimated tax payments on:",
 "100% of current-year tax", ["100% of prior-year tax", "90% of current-year tax", "110% of prior-year tax"],
 "A large corporation can use the prior year's tax for its first installment at most. It must otherwise pay 100% of the current year's tax to avoid the penalty."),
("A calendar-year C corporation's Form 1120 is due, without an extension, on:",
 "April 15", ["March 15", "May 15", "October 15"],
 "C corporation returns are due the 15th day of the 4th month after year end, April 15 for a calendar year. Partnership and S corporation returns are due March 15."),
("The business interest deduction is generally limited to business interest income plus what share of adjusted taxable income?",
 "30%", ["50%", "20%", "25%"],
 "Section 163(j) limits net business interest expense to 30% of adjusted taxable income plus floor plan interest. Disallowed interest carries forward."),
("A corporation must file Schedule M-3 instead of Schedule M-1 when its total assets at year end are at least:",
 "$10 million", ["$1 million", "$50 million", "$25 million"],
 "Corporations with total assets of $10 million or more on Schedule L must reconcile book and tax income on Schedule M-3."),
("Public Law 86-272 protects a seller from a state's net income tax when its only activity in the state is:",
 "Soliciting orders for goods", ["Providing repair services", "Licensing software there", "Leasing equipment there"],
 "P.L. 86-272 covers solicitation of orders for tangible personal property approved and shipped from outside the state. Services, licensing and leasing are not protected."),
("A corporation's State X factors are sales 40%, property 20% and payroll 30%. Under an equally weighted three-factor formula, how much of its $1,000,000 business income goes to State X?",
 "$300,000", ["$400,000", "$200,000", "$350,000"],
 "The average of the three factors is (40% + 20% + 30%) / 3 = 30%. $1,000,000 x 30% = $300,000."),
("A state uses a single sales factor. A corporation has $2 million of sales in that state, $8 million in total, and $600,000 of business income. The amount apportioned to the state is:",
 "$150,000", ["$200,000", "$75,000", "$450,000"],
 "The sales factor is $2 million / $8 million = 25%. $600,000 x 25% = $150,000."),
("Under UDITPA, nonbusiness dividend income is generally allocated to:",
 "The commercial domicile state", ["Every state by sales factor", "The payer's home state", "The state of incorporation"],
 "UDITPA allocates nonbusiness dividends and interest to the state of commercial domicile, the principal place from which the business is managed."),
("Which credit is a component of the general business credit for a C corporation?",
 "Research credit", ["Foreign tax credit", "Earned income credit", "Child tax credit"],
 "The research credit is part of the general business credit. The foreign tax credit is computed separately, and the other two are for individuals."),
]
